ncclsee-cpp 0e8433a: lock the event trees (applied by build.sh after cloning)

NCCL calls the profiler from the user thread and from its proxy thread. The proxy thread inserts KernelCh events
into their collective's children (Context::create_event_generic, an unlocked std::set) while the user thread stops
the collective inside ncclAllReduce and walks the whole tree for trace logging (EventBase::report_parent ->
report_children; the arguments are evaluated even with trace logging off). Concurrently, that is undefined
behavior; on the DGX Sparks, with every rank on 10 cores, ncclbench crashed in EventBase::report (SIGSEGV, address
0x90). With every rank on CPU 0 alone (mpirun's default binding before), the two threads hardly ever overlapped.

Fix: one recursive mutex per communicator (Context::tree_mtx), held while inserting children, while walking the
tree in report_parent (recursive: it recurses to the root), and while creating a group (the pool may flush there,
and its callback walks the trees of the finished groups). The tree walk itself stays, so ncclsee does the same work
per event as before, plus the lock. Upstream's unmerged branch refactor_writing (9209b75) fixes the same race by
locking the insertion and dropping report_parent altogether.

